// @expect-error Cannot read a member of 'Door?' because it may be null here. 'r.door' is a field, so it can change at any time. A later read of it can be null even after a null check. Copy it into a local first ('let door = r.door;') and check the local.
// @expect-error Cannot read a member of 'Door?' because it may be null here. 'doors[0]' is an array element, so it can change at any time. A later read of it can be null even after a null check. Copy it into a local first ('let value = doors[0];') and check the local.
// @expect-error Cannot read a member of 'Door?' because it may be null here. 'doors[i]' is an array element, so it can change at any time. A later read of it can be null even after a null check. Copy it into a local first ('let value = doors[i];') and check the local.
// @expect-error '+' needs numbers on both sides, but 'Settings.tally.count' has type 'int?'. 'Settings.tally.count' is a field, so it can change at any time. A later read of it can be null even after a null check. Copy it into a local first ('let count = Settings.tally.count;') and check the local.
// @expect-error build failed with 6 problems
// A mutable field and an array element are never stable places, because another reference can
// write either one between a check and a use. A null check on one proves nothing for the reads
// after it, and a write through one refines nothing. Each refusal names the step that is not
// stable and the local copy that is, at a literal index, at an identifier index and after a write.
// A mutable field reached through a lazy const is named the same way, and its local copy is
// spelled through the type name.
class Door {
    protected int code;
    protected constructor(this.code = 0);
}

class Room {
    protected Door? door;
    protected constructor(this.door = null);
}

class Tally {
    protected int? count;
    protected constructor(this.count = null);
}

checkedField(Room r) -> int {
    if (r.door != null) {
        return r.door.code;          // refused after a check on a field
    }
    return 0;
}

writtenField(Room r) -> int {
    r.door = new Door(8);
    return r.door.code;              // refused after a write through a field
}

checkedElement(Door?[] doors) -> int {
    if (doors[0] != null) {
        return doors[0].code;        // refused after a check on an element
    }
    return 0;
}

checkedElementByName(Door?[] doors, int i) -> int {
    if (doors[i] != null) {
        return doors[i].code;        // refused the same way at an identifier index
    }
    return 0;
}

writtenElement(Door?[] doors) -> int {
    doors[0] = new Door(9);
    return doors[0].code;            // refused after a write through an element
}

main() -> int {
    Door?[] doors = [new Door(1)];
    return checkedField(new Room()) + writtenField(new Room()) + checkedElement(doors)
        + checkedElementByName(doors, 0) + writtenElement(doors);
}

class Settings {
    protected lazy const Tally tally = new Tally(1);
}

lazyRooted() -> int {
    if (Settings.tally.count != null) {
        return Settings.tally.count + 1;   // a mutable field under a lazy const
    }
    return 0;
}
